    qjsm -l | grep -i stream

- import-so-suffix-misses-installed (src/qjsm.c): `import 'path.so'` does
  not reach an installed `path.so` when `path` is also builtin; the builtin
  shadows it and there is no spelling for the installed file (see
  doc/qjsm-layout.md 4).

    qjsm -e 'import("path.so").then(m=>print(Object.keys(m).length))'

- misc-classid-lookup-finds-nothing (src/utils.c:1356, js_class_find): the
  runtime class-table scan returns -1 for every name, built-in classes
  included, so `misc.classId()` is useless with the installed libquickjs
  (1.4). js_class_array() cannot locate class_array inside JSRuntime.
  found while trying to resolve the ArrayBufferSink class id from
  src/stream-utils.c by name.

    import { classId } from 'misc';
    classId('Object');
    // expected: a number
    // actual: undefined

- syscallerror-no-code-for-eloop (quickjs-syscallerror.c): SyscallError
  built with errno 40 has `code === null` and no `SyscallError.ELOOP`
  constant, though the message is right. found while writing lib/vfs.js,
  which hardcodes 40.

    import { SyscallError } from 'syscallerror';
    new SyscallError('stat', 40).code;
    // expected: 'ELOOP'
    // actual: null

- lexer-imports-util-by-file-path (lib/lexer/cpp.js:6,
  lib/lexer/ecmascript.js:1): both import '../util.js', a file path, but
  util is a builtin compiled into qjsm and `cmake --install` leaves
  util.js out of lib/quickjs. In an installation without a stale
  util.js (a fresh DESTDIR, a new prefix) lexer/cpp, lexer/ecmascript and
  everything that imports them fail to load: lexer/c, lexer/bnf,
  lexers, c-functions, parser/cpp. /usr/local only works because an old
  install left util.js there. found by utilities/api-surface.sh.

    // QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH=<fresh DESTDIR>/usr/local/lib/quickjs
    import 'lexer/cpp.js';
    // expected: loads (a bare 'util' resolves to the builtin)
    // actual: ReferenceError: could not load module '.../util.js'

- xmlserializer-read-n-splits-utf8 (quickjs-xml.c, js_xmlserializer_read):
  read(n) in string mode cuts the output at n bytes and decodes each piece
  on its own, so a multi-byte character that straddles a cut becomes
  U+FFFD in both pieces; the concatenation differs from read(1000).
  found by tests/tools/xml-write-golden.js.

    const s = new XMLSerializer([{tagName: 'a', children: ['ü€']}]);
    let out = '', c;
    while((c = s.read(1)) !== '') out += c;
    // expected: '<a>ü€</a>'
    // actual: replacement characters in place of ü€

- path-exists-true-for-dangling-symlink (quickjs-path.c, PATH_EXISTS ->
  path_exists1 in src/path.c): exists() uses lstat(), so a symlink whose
  target is missing counts as existing. fs.existsSync(), node:fs, Bun and
  Deno follow the link and answer false. src/qjsm.c and the module search
  also call path_exists1, so a fix needs a separate JS-facing stat() check.

    import * as path from 'path';
    import * as fs from 'fs';
    // ln -s /nowhere /tmp/dl
    path.exists('/tmp/dl');     // expected: false (as fs.existsSync)
    fs.existsSync('/tmp/dl');   // false
    // actual: path.exists is true

- path-missing-node-names (quickjs-path.c): path.win32, matchesGlob and
  the specifier path/win32 (node:path/win32) do not exist. Node, Bun and
  Deno have them. See doc/api-compare-path.md; plan in TODO.md Tier 22
  (phases W and G).

    import win32 from 'path/win32';
    // expected: the win32 path object
    // actual: ReferenceError: could not load module 'path/win32'


- xml-doctype-internal-subset-mangled (src/xml.c, bang-tag scan): a
  DOCTYPE with an internal subset ends at the first '>' inside the
  subset, so the subset is split into a bogus tag name and a text node
  "]>", and entities declared in it are not available. found while
  writing doc/api-compare-xml.md.

    xml.read('<!DOCTYPE a [<!ENTITY foo "val">]><a>&foo;</a>');
    // expected: a !DOCTYPE node holding the whole declaration
    // actual: [{tagName: '!DOCTYPE a [<!ENTITY foo "val"'}, ']>', {tagName: 'a', ...}]

- jsonparser-error-position-after-token (src/json.c, quickjs-json.c:1560):
  JsonParser reports the position after the offending token, one column
  right of JSON.parse and json5 (2:8 for a bad token at 2:7); the error
  carries the position only in its message.

    new JsonParser('{"a":\n  [1, x]}', 'f.json').parse() // ... throws
    // expected: message ends 'f.json:2:7'
    // actual: 'f.json:2:8: expected a value'

- xmlpushparser-silent-on-mismatch (quickjs-xml.c, xread_callback):
  XMLPushParser.write() accepts a mismatched end tag without an error and
  the position of a later close() failure is not reported.

    const p = new XMLPushParser(); p.write('<a>\n <b></c></a>'); p.close();
    // expected: SyntaxError at 2:5
    // actual: no error, p.root holds a repaired tree


- resolve-imports-use-strict-ends-import-scan: utilities/resolve-imports.js
  stops collecting imports at the first `;` that is not part of an
  import (line 2161 sets doneImports), so a file that starts with a
  "use strict"; directive has none of its imports followed by
  --recursive. Every file of commonmark.js starts that way. The same pass
  also does not follow `export { default as X } from './x.js'`, and
  --remove-exports keeps the export keywords of the inlined files.

    printf '"use strict";\nimport { a } from "./b.js";\n' > /tmp/a.js
    printf 'export const a = 1;\n' > /tmp/b.js
    resolve-imports.js --recursive --print-files -o /dev/null /tmp/a.js
    # prints only /tmp/a.js, without the directive also /tmp/b.js

- lexer-eof-false-after-last-token: for a string input, Lexer.next()
  returns null once the input is consumed, but lexer.eof stays false,
  so a loop on !lexer.eof never ends (doc/native/lexer.md says eof is
  true once everything was consumed).

    import { Lexer } from 'lexer';
    const l = new Lexer('ab', Lexer.LONGEST);
    l.addRule('t', /[a-z]+/);
    l.next(); console.log(l.next(), l.eof);   // null false

- qjsm-no-atob-btoa: qjsm has no WHATWG atob/btoa globals (and no
  Buffer), so npm packages that decode base64 tables at import time fail,
  e.g. entities 7: ReferenceError: 'Buffer' is not defined in
  decodeBase64. lib/node/buffer.js is the only base64 code in lib/.

    qjsm -e 'console.log(typeof atob, typeof btoa)'   # undefined undefined

- lexer-cpp-no-continuation-token: test-lexer-cpp.js 'an escaped newline
  continues the directive' fails: lib/lexer/c.js lexes the backslash of a
  continued #define line as no token at all, the test expects a
  pp_continuation token between pp_identifier:A and pp_number:1. Found
  2026-10-20 while running the lexer tests for lib/lexer/ecmascript.js,
  fails the same with that change undone.

    cd tests/unittests && qjsm test-lexer-cpp.js
    # 'pp_hash:#,pp_directive:define,pp_identifier:A,pp_number:1,...'
    # expected '...,pp_identifier:A,pp_continuation:\,pp_number:1,...'

- readfilesync-procfs-empty: fs.readFileSync on a /proc file returns an
  empty string (std.loadFile too), because the size comes from stat, which
  is 0 there. node reads to EOF. Found 2026-10-20.

    qjsm -e "import('fs').then(fs => console.log(fs.readFileSync('/proc/self/status', 'utf8').length))"
    # 0, node: about 1500

- io-getline-destructures-string: lib/io.js IOReadDecorator.getline()
  destructures the decoded string as [code, len] and passes the first
  character to String.fromCodePoint, which throws RangeError for a letter
  and gives a control character for a digit.
  It also decodes partial sequences without stream: true. Found 2026-10-20.

- ecmascript-lexer-escaped-identifier: lib/lexer/ecmascript.js does not lex
  an identifier with a unicode escape, valid JS since ES2015: a and
  \u{62} give UNEXPECTED_CHARACTER at the backslash. IdentifierStart has
  "\\"[u]{HexDigit}{4}, which the native lexer apparently reads as the
  literal text, and the \u{...} form is missing. module-lexer reports the
  error and goes on. Found 2026-10-20.

    import { parse } from 'lib/module-lexer.js';
    parse('var \\u0061 = 1').error
    // { code: 'UNEXPECTED_CHARACTER', line: 1, column: 5 }, expected none

- fs-readfilesync-directory-rangeerror: lib/fs.js readFileSync() on a
  directory throws RangeError: invalid array index from new ArrayBuffer
  (fs.js:706), because tell() after seeking to the end of a directory is
  not a usable size. node throws an Error with code EISDIR. Found
  2026-10-20.

    import * as fs from 'fs';
    fs.readFileSync('/tmp');   // RangeError: invalid array index

- resolve-imports-bundle-name-clash: the bundler concatenates files without
  renaming top-level names, so two files that declare the same name give a
  bundle that does not load. Found 2026-10-20 bundling magic-string, whose
  index.mjs and @jridgewell/sourcemap-codec both declare intToChar.

    resolve-imports.js -o b.mjs ../../magic-string/dist/index.mjs
    qjsm b.mjs   # SyntaxError: invalid redefinition of global identifier

- builtin-modules-cache-pins-defaults: cmake/qjsm.cmake caches
  BUILTIN_MODULES on the first configure, so a builtin added to the
  defaults later (loaders/packages) never reaches an existing build
  directory. The same was fixed for BUILTINS_COMPILED only. Workaround:
  cmake -U BUILTIN_MODULES <builddir>. Found 2026-10-20.

- directory-options-object-zeroes-defaults: new Directory(path, { recursive })
  and new Directory({ path }) set flags and mask from the options object
  with js_get_propertystr_int32, which gives 0 for a missing property, so
  the defaults FLAG_BOTH and TYPE_MASK are lost and next() yields entries
  without name or type. Found 2026-10-21.

    new Directory('/tmp', { recursive: false }).next()   // flags 0

- typescript-test-gated-on-swc-cli: test-typescript.js runs only when the
  swc CLI is in PATH, but lib/loaders/typescript.js calls node's
  module.stripTypeScriptTypes, so a box with node 22.13+ and no swc skips
  the suite and one with swc and an old node fails it. doc/js/README.md
  still says the loader goes through swc. Found 2026-10-21.

    PATH=/usr/bin qjsm tests/unittests/test-typescript.js
    # SKIP: swc not found in PATH, although node would do

- extract-structs-finds-no-function-lists: utilities/extract-structs.js
  prints cFuncListObjects {} and [] for every input, even quickjs-path.c,
  whose JSCFunctionListEntry tables and JS_SetPropertyFunctionList calls
  it is meant to collect. Not the pp_* lexer change alone: a file without
  any directive gives the same empty result. Its rules['preprocessor']
  test (line 213) is dead since CLexer has no such rule. Found 2026-10-21.

    qjsm utilities/extract-structs.js quickjs-path.c
    # cFuncListObjects {}
    # []

- require-ignores-package-exports: lib/require.js finds a package's file
  by package.json main, index.js, main.js only, so a package with only
  "exports" is a directory, but not a package (@typescript-eslint/types),
  and a subpath like require('eslint/use-at-your-own-risk') is not found.
  Fix: resolve bare names with packageResolve() of lib/loaders/packages.js
  and the conditions node, require. Found 2026-10-21.

    require('@typescript-eslint/types');
    # qjsm: Error: Module .../@typescript-eslint/types is a directory,
    #       but not a package

- require-no-json-extension: lookupModule() of lib/require.js adds .js to
  an extensionless path but not .json, so require('./data') of a data.json
  is not found (core-js-compat). Node tries X, X.js, X.json, X/index.js,
  X/index.json. Fix: try .json too and load it with loadJSON(). Found
  2026-10-21.

    require('core-js-compat/data');
    # qjsm: Error: Module .../core-js-compat/data not found!

- require-resolve-missing: require.resolve() does not exist, so
  init-package-json fails with not a function. Fix: require.resolve(spec)
  returns what lookupModule(spec, dir) finds and throws an Error with code
  MODULE_NOT_FOUND otherwise, require.resolve.paths(spec) the
  nodeModulesPaths() list. Found 2026-10-21.

    typeof require.resolve
    # node: 'function'   qjsm: 'undefined'

- commonjs-require-not-per-module: lib/loaders/commonjs.js passes every
  module the one global require and sets require.filename only while the
  module body runs. A relative require() made later, from a function or
  getter, resolves against the main script's directory (enhanced-resolve's
  lazy getters: Module <work dir>/ResolverFactory not found). Fix: give
  run() a require bound to its path, Node's createRequire(path), with
  resolve, cache and main on it. Found 2026-10-21.

    // sub/lib.cjs   module.exports =
    //                 { get y() { return require('./y.cjs'); } };
    // sub/y.cjs     module.exports = 1;
    (await import('./sub/lib.cjs')).default.y;
    # node: 1   qjsm: Error: Module <main script dir>/y.cjs not found!

- commonjs-stack-has-no-file-and-line: lib/loaders/commonjs.js compiles
  a module with new Function(...), so its frames read <input> and the
  line is 2 too high. Fix: evalBuf('(function (exports, require, module,
  __filename, __dirname) {' + source + '\n})', path) keeps the source on
  its own lines under the file's name, and throws the same SyntaxError for
  an ES module. Found 2026-10-21.

    // x.cjs   line 1, then: throw new Error('here');   on line 2
    # node: at Object.<anonymous> (/dir/x.cjs:2:7)
    # qjsm: at anonymous (<input>:4:7)

- quickjs-regexp-v-flag-hyphen: the QuickJS that qjsm links rejects a
  plain - anywhere in a pattern with the v flag, also outside a class:
  /a-b/v, /^(?:-?\d+n)/v throw invalid character in class (5 packages:
  jsdoc-type-pratt-parser, eslint-plugin-jsdoc, eslint-plugin-regexp, ...).
  get_class_atom() in ../quickjs/libregexp.c checks the reserved and the
  doubled punctuators under s->unicode_sets alone. Fix: s->unicode_sets &&
  inclass in both checks. Also inside a class v mode allows the escaped
  punctuators & ! # % , : ; < = > @ ` ~ (ClassSetReservedPunctuator),
  which throw invalid escape sequence. Found 2026-10-21.

    /a-b/v;          // node: ok   qjsm: SyntaxError
    /[a\&]/v;        // node: ok   qjsm: SyntaxError

- misc-btoa-ignores-view-offset: misc.btoa() of a subarray or DataView
  encodes the whole underlying ArrayBuffer, and btoa(x, true) (base64url)
  still pads with =. js_input_chars in quickjs-misc.c takes the buffer
  without byteOffset and byteLength. Found 2026-10-21.

    import { btoa } from 'misc';
    const u = new Uint8Array([0xfb, 0xff, 1, 2]);
    btoa(u.subarray(0, 2));        // '+/8BAg==', expected '+/8='
    btoa(u.subarray(0, 2), true);  // '-_8BAg==', expected '-_8'

- process-arch-x32-for-ia32: process.arch (lib/process.js) maps an i386
  to i686 machine to 'x32', Node says 'ia32' there (x32 is the 64-bit ABI
  with 32-bit pointers), and armv7l stays 'armv7l' where Node says 'arm'.
  os.arch() of node:os returns process.arch, so it inherits both. Found
  2026-10-21.

    // on an i686 kernel
    process.arch;    // node: 'ia32'   qjsm: 'x32'

- builtin-modules-cache-pins-default: a new name in qjsm.cmake's default
  BUILTINS_COMPILED list does not reach an existing build dir. The first
  configure caches the expanded default as BUILTIN_MODULES, and every
  later configure keeps it, so the new builtin is missing until
  -UBUILTIN_MODULES. qjsm_init_builtins() drops a cached BUILTINS_COMPILED
  default by its help string, but not the BUILTIN_MODULES it built. Found
  2026-10-21.

    # add node/os to BUILTINS_COMPILED in cmake/qjsm.cmake, then
    cmake -B build/x86_64-linux-gnu -S .    # node/os not listed

- fs-readfilesync-proc-empty: fs.readFileSync() of a /proc or /sys file
  returns '' (or an empty buffer), Node returns the contents. These files
  report size 0, so a read sized by stat() or fseek/ftell gets nothing,
  std.loadFile() does the same. Read until EOF instead. Found 2026-10-21.

    import { readFileSync } from 'fs';
    readFileSync('/proc/loadavg', 'utf8');  // node: '1.84 ...'  qjsm: ''

- node-os-networkinterfaces-unimplemented: os.networkInterfaces() of
  node:os throws InternalError, qjs-modules has no getifaddrs(3) binding.
  Needs misc.getifaddrs() (name, family, address, netmask, MAC from
  AF_PACKET, scope id), then lib/node/os.js builds Node's { address,
  netmask, family, mac, internal, cidr, scopeid }. TODO.md 29.1. Found
  2026-10-21.

    import os from 'node:os';
    os.networkInterfaces();   // node: { lo: [...], ... }  qjsm: InternalError

- node-os-setpriority-unimplemented: os.setPriority() of node:os checks
  pid and priority as Node does, then throws InternalError, there is no
  setpriority(2) binding. Needs misc.setpriority(which, who, prio), its
  errno mapped to Node's SystemError (EACCES, ESRCH). TODO.md 29.2. Found
  2026-10-21.

    import os from 'node:os';
    os.setPriority(10);       // node: undefined  qjsm: InternalError

- node-os-userinfo-passwd-only: os.userInfo() of node:os, and homedir()
  without $HOME, read /etc/passwd only. A user that NSS provides (LDAP,
  sssd, systemd-homed) has no line there, so userInfo() throws SystemError
  ENOENT where Node returns the entry. Needs misc.getpwuid(uid) on
  getpwuid_r(3). TODO.md 29.3. Found 2026-10-21.

    import os from 'node:os';
    os.userInfo();            // ldap user, node: { username, ... }
                              // qjsm: SystemError ERR_SYSTEM_ERROR ENOENT

- node-os-linux-proc-only: cpus(), freemem(), totalmem(), loadavg(),
  uptime() and getPriority() of node:os read /proc, so they throw
  InternalError on macOS, BSD and Windows, and availableParallelism()
  falls back to cpus() there and throws too. Needs sysctl(3),
  GetSystemTimes and the like behind one misc.sysinfo(). TODO.md 29.4.
  Found 2026-10-21.

    import os from 'node:os';
    os.totalmem();            // macOS, node: 17179869184
                              // qjsm: InternalError, no /proc/meminfo

- misc-gettid-never-detected: misc exports gettid only #if HAVE_GETTID,
  config.h.cmake has #cmakedefine HAVE_GETTID since 2026-10-13, but no
  check_functions() call tests for gettid, so it is never defined and
  test-misc.js fails "not a function". Found 2026-10-21.

    qjsm -e 'import("misc").then(m => console.log(typeof m.gettid))'
                              # undefined on glibc 2.30+

- misc-valuepointer-not-bigint: misc.valuePointer({}) returns a number,
  test-misc.js expects a bigint (typeof p === 'bigint'), so the test
  fails. js_newpointer() picks the type. Found 2026-10-21.

    qjsm -e 'import("misc").then(m => console.log(typeof m.valuePointer({})))'
                              # number

- os-ttygetwinsize-win32-buffer-size: quickjs-libc's os.ttyGetWinSize() on
  Windows returns the console screen buffer size (dwSize, e.g. [120, 9001])
  instead of the visible window (srWindow), so a full-screen redraw or a
  line wrap computed from it is wrong on Windows. lib/ no longer calls it,
  misc.ttyGetWinSize(fd) reports the window. The fix belongs in
  ../quickjs-libc.c js_os_ttyGetWinSize (srWindow.Right - Left + 1, Bottom -
  Top + 1).

    os.ttyGetWinSize(1)     // [120, 9001] in a 120x30 cmd.exe window
    misc.ttyGetWinSize(1)   // [120, 30]

- deep-equals-leaks-pending-rangeerror: deep.equals() returns false and leaves
  "RangeError: PropertyEnumeration is at end" pending when one side runs out
  of properties first, or has none to enumerate: {a: undefined} vs {}, two
  equal Dates, two RegExps, two Maps, Object.create(null) vs {}, two
  ArrayBuffers. A try/catch around the call does not see it, the error
  surfaces at the next exception check. Bun.deepEquals does not use it (own
  walk in lib/bun/util.js).

    import { equals } from 'deep';
    try { equals({ a: undefined }, {}); } catch(e) { /* not reached */ }
    // RangeError: PropertyEnumeration is at end, after the try

- quickjs-local-function-overwrites-import: in QuickJS (qjs and qjsm), a
  hoisted function declaration with the name of an import replaces the
  exporting module's binding for every importer, not only for the module
  that declares it. Node and the spec reject it as a SyntaxError. lib/bun.js
  (resolve, from path) and lib/repl.js (define, from util) had it, both
  fixed by dropping or renaming the import. A check in a lint script would
  catch new cases.

    // lib.js:  export function f() { return 'lib'; }
    // a.js:    import { f } from './lib.js'; function f() { return 'a'; }
    // main.js: import './a.js'; import { f } from './lib.js'; f()  // 'a'

- jsonserializer-iterator-never-done (quickjs-json.c, JsonSerializer
  [Symbol.iterator]): after the last chunk the iterator returns
  {done: false} with no value forever, so spread or for...of hangs.
  found while writing the doc/native/json.md example.

    [...new JsonSerializer([1])]
    // expected: ['[1]']
    // actual: never returns
